## 4. The Shell

### 4.1 What the Shell Is

The shell is the "depth" of the mode in the mesh:

| Shell | Particles | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | ν | Unbound — at the surface |
| 1 | e, u, d | First layer — lightly bound |
| 1.5 | s, μ, dark | Intermediate layer |
| 2 | c, τ, b | Second layer — more deeply bound |
| 3 | W, Z, H, t | Core layer — most deeply bound |

### 4.2 The Shell Operator

The shell is the eigenvalue of an operator of the form:

```
O = Σ_site w(site) |site⟩⟨site|
```

where `w(site)` is a weight function on the 64 sites. The shell of mode `i` is:

```
shell_i = Σ_site w(site) · |v_i(site)|²
```

### 4.3 The Weight Function

The least-squares weight function `w` has:

- A large constant component (Fourier mode (0,0))
- Significant contributions from many other Fourier modes
- No simple symmetry
- No separability

**The weight function is not unique.** Any `w` satisfying `A @ w = shell` works. The least-squares solution is one choice.

